{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Hoy.</b> Some interpret the heh in place of alef, like אדורם (1 Kings 12:18) / הדורם (2 Chronicles 10:18), איך (Exodus 2:6, 9) and היך (1 Chronicles 1:12). In my opinion it is a sign of a call, and the root of the word is from היה, because we find הוי הוי ונוסו (Zechariah 2:10), הוי ציון המלטי (ibid. 11); therefore after it he said לנוכח על מה תכו:" "<b>Heavy of iniquity.</b> A descriptive noun in construct state, like ערל לב and ערל בשר (Ezekiel 44:7)." "<b>Seed of evildoers.</b> Because they themselves together with their fathers are evildoers. Or the meaning is like \"your father was an Amorite and your mother a Hittite\" (Ezekiel 16:3), as I shall explain." "<b>Corrupters.</b> Their souls or their ways." "<b>They have forsaken YHWH.</b> The meaning is the worship of HaShem." "<b>They have spurned.</b> They have provoked." "<b>They have drawn back.</b> From the root וינזרו מקדשי (Leviticus 22:2), on the pattern of ולא יכולו (Exodus 8:14). And if it is not in a recognized form, Rabbi [?] said that it is from the nif'al binyan, like נכונו ללצים (Proverbs 29:1 [MT 19:29]), from the root זר."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The colon after תכו is retained because the verse citation appears truncated in the source; it likely refers to לנוכח על מה תכה (Isaiah 1:11 or similar).", 1 "The citation is given as Ezekiel 44:6, but ערל בשר appears at Ezekiel 44:7; retained as in source.", 6 "The citation נכונו ללצים appears at Proverbs 19:29 in MT, though the source gives no verse number; resolved by context. 'Rabbi [?]' — רבי' appears to be an abbreviation, possibly Rabbi Isaac or another figure; left unresolved."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:30.053541069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}